    {
      "n": 2,
      "text": "second seat on the same machine; measured by the lead with the fixed benches, and read by the second seat. T6's requirement is reworded, which narrows what T6-c03 shows: first import is no longer a ceiling the old code must fail. On a 16-core Linux server the old code took 60.6 s against a ceiling of 200 s, so that ceiling cannot catch it there, and the contract's own rule is that a ceiling the old code stands under twice of on the fastest machine is a guard. The gain measured on the iMac, 405 s to 90 s, stays in docs/testing/SPEED-RESULTS.md, and the ceiling stays as a guard. The seven other ceilings stay in CAUGHT, each at 3.1 times or more on that server. The new text: The ceilings sit well above what the iMac measured, so a slower computer passes. Those in CAUGHT sit under the old cost. a4_new_note_p50_ms and a8_stop_ms are guards the old code also passes, and claim no gain. a3_recall_p95_ms, a6_send_ms and a7_first_import_ms are guards as well: the old code measured under twice their ceilings on the fastest machine this runs on, so they are not counted as catching it. Seven ceilings catch the old code.",
